{
  "analysis": [
    {
      "layout": "s0A",
      "strengths": "Complete part numbers and suppliers are easy to scan, and descriptions fit in two lines without breaking words. Consistent row heights support comparison.",
      "faults": "The table requires substantial horizontal scrolling. The initial viewport shows complete part numbers but only fragments of descriptions, with quantity, price and supplier out of sight.",
      "spatial_logic": "The generous description width creates an even, compact grid, though it separates identifiers from purchasing details across a wide table."
    },
    {
      "layout": "s0C",
      "strengths": "Quantity and price remain legible, and almost the entire table width lies within the viewport.",
      "faults": "Part numbers, descriptions and especially suppliers break within words. The supplier header becomes nearly vertical, and the slight horizontal overflow still requires scrolling. Tall rows make lookup and comparison arduous.",
      "spatial_logic": "A few pixels saved in width impose extreme height and fragmentation, with large empty areas beside the longest wrapped cells."
    },
    {
      "layout": "P",
      "strengths": "Like s0A, it preserves identifiers and suppliers, keeps descriptions to two lines and gives the rows a steady rhythm. It is slightly narrower.",
      "faults": "The description wraps some units awkwardly, including separating 72 from MHz and 5.25 from V. Purchasing columns remain beyond the initial viewport.",
      "spatial_logic": "Its compact, uniform rows are useful for comparison, but the narrower description saves little scrolling relative to s0A and produces less natural breaks."
    },
    {
      "layout": "S",
      "strengths": "It reduces horizontal travel while retaining whole identifiers and supplier names. Most descriptions remain within two lines.",
      "faults": "The microcontroller row grows to three lines and separates 20 KB from RAM; other breaks also interrupt technical phrases. The initial viewport still hides the purchasing columns.",
      "spatial_logic": "A narrower description trades some vertical regularity for reduced width. The one taller row is acceptable if the shorter horizontal journey is valued."
    },
    {
      "layout": "Q",
      "strengths": "All five columns are accessible without horizontal scrolling, and quantities and prices remain readable.",
      "faults": "Part numbers and supplier names fragment across lines, as do words in the description and headers. Tall, uneven rows make it difficult to scan a BOM or associate purchasing details with an identifier.",
      "spatial_logic": "The fit is achieved by compressing the text-bearing columns below their content needs, producing a long table with considerable empty space in the numeric columns."
    }
  ],
  "insights": "This BOM needs intact part numbers for reliable lookup and enough description width for technical phrases. The scrolling layouts preserve those functions much better, but their first viewport exposes only part of the description and none of the purchasing columns. A viewport-fitting layout cannot accommodate every longest unbreakable unit: the measured minima plus padding already exceed the available width. Within that constraint, width should be assigned deliberately to the most important lookup fields and to avoiding the worst word fragmentation.",
